{"step_index":1066,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:27:32Z","thinking":"**Understanding Roman Dominance Inequality**\n\nConsider a Roman dominating function *f* and the set of vertices *V* with positive values, denoted *V<sub>>0</sub>*. Observe that vertices with value 0 must be \"protected\" by vertices with value 2.  The goal is to connect the radius of the graph to the total \"weight\" (sum of function values) of the function.\n\nThe radius relates to how far a vertex can be from any center, while the Roman weight is the sum of the function values assigned to vertices. The key idea is every un-protected vertex by the function is adjacent to a vertex in *V<sub>2</sub>*. It is suspected, this property and connectedness will connect the radius and roman weight.\n\n\n**Bounding Graph Radius via Roman Weight**\n\nEvery vertex is within distance 1 of $V_{>0}$, implying the graph's vertex set is covered by balls of radius 1 centered on $V_{>0}$. Since the cardinality of $V_{>0}$ is less than or equal to the roman weight, a bound on the graph's radius can be derived.\n\nThe radius of the graph is known to be no more than the number of vertices minus 1. If the graph's vertex count is less than or equal to the Roman weight, the desired inequality is already established: radius + 1 <= Roman weight. If roman weight is less than vertex count, more analysis is needed.\n\n\n**Analyzing Distance in the Graph**\n\n  The average function value being less than 1 implies some vertices have value 0, and thus, some vertex has value 2 to dominate these 0-valued vertices. Considering the case where $|V_2| = 1$ (exactly one vertex with value 2, call it *u*).  Every 0-valued vertex must be adjacent to *u*.\n\n  Focusing on vertices with value 1 (*V<sub>1</sub>*), the Roman weight is 2 + |*V<sub>1</sub>*|. Any shortest path from *u* to any other vertex has length at most 1, thus radius <= 1.\n\n  Since all vertices not in *V<sub>1</sub>* are within distance 1 of *u*, the maximum distance to any vertex occurs between *u* and some vertex in *V<sub>1</sub>*. Further investigation is needed to accurately calculate the distance bound f\n<truncated 5046 bytes>\n graphs with radius 4, if the weight is <= 4, then the radius must be <= 3. For graphs with radius 4 or more, the Roman weight must be >= 5, so radius + 1 (5) <= Roman weight.\n\nFor a graph with radius 5, a Roman weight of <= 5 would force a radius <= 4; therefore, when the radius is >= 5, the Roman weight is >= 6, and thus radius + 1 (6) <= Roman weight. If n<=6 then the radius <=5, covering all graphs.\n\n**Considering Induction Approach**\n\nBy induction, it appears for any *k*, if the Roman weight <= *k*, then the radius <= *k*-1. Thus, to prove the goal `radius(G) + 1 <= romanWeight(f)` for any Roman domination function *f*, the core idea is if  `romanWeight(f) <= k` then `radius(G) <= k-1`.\n\n\n**Connecting Radius to Roman Domination Number**\n\nThe goal changes to proving `GraphCalc.radius G + 1 ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G`. It is recalled `radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V` from `test_card_bound2.lean`.\n\nThe relationship between `Fintype.card V` and `romanDominationNumber G` is considered. It is considered whether `Fintype.card V ≤ romanDominationNumber G` is always true.\n\nA counterexample is constructed using a star graph K<sub>1,n-1</sub>. In this case, `romanDominationNumber` is 2, while the vertex count n, thus `Fintype.card V > romanDominationNumber`. It is noted that, in the star graph, `radius + 1 = 2 = romanDominationNumber`.\n\n\n**Connecting High Degree to Radius and Weight**\n\nHigh-degree vertices, labeled with 2, cover many vertices reducing the graph's radius while minimizing the Roman domination weight. Low radius necessitates a \"thin\" graph with small degrees, which increases the Roman domination number.  The core idea is, `radius + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber`.\n\nAttempting to formalize this using Lean 4 by checking available theorems in files such as `test_ball.lean`, `test_card.lean`, and `test_weight.lean`. Specifically, reviewing theorems in `test_card_bound2.lean` to assess if current tools are sufficient. Examining  `two_le_romanWeight` and other available lemmas to prove the theorem.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"cat << 'EOF' > /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/scratch_rdn.lean\\nimport Mathlib\\nimport LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\\n\\nopen SimpleGraph\\nopen GraphCalc\\n\\nlemma two_le_romanWeight {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\\n    (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) (f : V → Fin 3)\\n    (hf : GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) :\\n    2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanWeight f := by\\n  unfold GraphCalc.romanWeight\\n  unfold GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass at hnt\\n  unfold GraphCalc.order at hnt\\n  by_contra hc\\n  have h_le1 : ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≤ 1 := by omega\\n  have h_no2 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 2 := by\\n    intro v hv\\n    have h_mem := Finset.single_le_sum (fun u _ => Nat.zero_le (f u : ℕ)) (Finset.mem_univ v)\\n    rw [hv] at h_mem\\n    omega\\n  have h_no0 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 0 := by\\n    intro v hv\\n    have h_f0 : f v = 0 := by\\n      ext\\n      exact hv\\n    obtain ⟨u, _, hu2⟩ := hf v h_f0\\n    have hu2_nat : (f u : ℕ) = 2 := by\\n      rw [hu2]\\n      rfl\\n    exact h_no2 u hu2_nat\\n  have h_ge1 : ∀ v : V, 1 ≤ (f v : ℕ) := by\\n    intro v\\n    have h0 := h_no0 v\\n    omega\\n  have h_sum_ge : ∑ v : V, 1 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) :=\\n    Finset.sum_le_sum (fun v _ => h_ge1 v)\\n  rw [Finset.sum_const, nsmul_eq_mul, mul_one] at h_sum_ge\\n  rw [Finset.card_univ] at h_sum_ge\\n  have h2 : 2 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) := hnt.trans h_sum_ge\\n  omega\\n\\nlemma two_le_romanDominationNumber {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\\n    (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) :\\n    2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G := by\\n  unfold GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber\\n  let S := (Finset.univ.filter (GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G)).image GraphCalc.romanWeight\\n  have hconst : (fun _ : V => (2 : Fin 3)) ∈ Finset.univ.filter (GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G) := by\\n    rw [Finset.mem_filter]\\n    refine ⟨Finset.mem_univ _, ?_⟩\\n    int\n<truncated 548 bytes>","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"20000","toolAction":"\"Testing two_le_romanDominationNumber\"","toolSummary":"\"Check scratch_rdn.lean\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking","tool_calls"]}
{"step_index":1067,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"RUNNING","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:27:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:27:54+02:00\nTool is running as a background task with task id: f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/task-1067\nTask Description: cat << 'EOF' > /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/scratch_rdn.lean\nimport Mathlib\nimport LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\n\nopen SimpleGraph\nopen GraphCalc\n\nlemma two_le_romanWeight {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) (f : V → Fin 3)\n    (hf : GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) :\n    2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanWeight f := by\n  unfold GraphCalc.romanWeight\n  unfold GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass at hnt\n  unfold GraphCalc.order at hnt\n  by_contra hc\n  have h_le1 : ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≤ 1 := by omega\n  have h_no2 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 2 := by\n    intro v hv\n    have h_mem := Finset.single_le_sum (fun u _ => Nat.zero_le (f u : ℕ)) (Finset.mem_univ v)\n    rw [hv] at h_mem\n    omega\n  have h_no0 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 0 := by\n    intro v hv\n    have h_f0 : f v = 0 := by\n      ext\n      exact hv\n    obtain ⟨u, _, hu2⟩ := hf v h_f0\n    have hu2_nat : (f u : ℕ) = 2 := by\n      rw [hu2]\n      rfl\n    exact h_no2 u hu2_nat\n  have h_ge1 : ∀ v : V, 1 ≤ (f v : ℕ) := by\n    intro v\n    have h0 := h_no0 v\n    omega\n  have h_sum_ge : ∑ v : V, 1 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) :=\n    Finset.sum_le_sum (fun v _ => h_ge1 v)\n  rw [Finset.sum_const, nsmul_eq_mul, mul_one] at h_sum_ge\n  rw [Finset.card_univ] at h_sum_ge\n  have h2 : 2 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) := hnt.trans h_sum_ge\n  omega\n\nlemma two_le_romanDominationNumber {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) :\n    2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G := by\n  unfold GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber\n  let S := (Finset.univ.filter (GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G)).image GraphCalc.romanWeight\n  have hconst : (fun _ : V => (2 : Fin 3)) ∈ Finset.univ.filter (GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G) := by\n    rw [Finset.mem_filter]\n    refine ⟨Finset.mem_univ _, ?_⟩\n    intro v hv\n    revert hv\n    decide\n  have hSne : S.Nonempty := by\n    use GraphCalc.romanWeight (fun _ => 2)\n    apply Finset.mem_image_of_mem\n    exact hconst\n  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := Finset.min_of_nonempty hSne\n  rw [hm]\n  simp only [Option.getD_some]\n  have hmem : m ∈ S := Finset.mem_of_min hm\n  rw [Finset.mem_image] at hmem\n  obtain ⟨f, hf, rfl⟩ := hmem\n  rw [Finset.mem_filter] at hf\n  exact two_le_romanWeight G hnt f hf.2\n\nEOF\n/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/check.sh scratch_rdn.lean\n\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/tasks/task-1067.log\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO ACTIONS: A) either proceed to other relevant work (if any) or, B) simply update the user with a short message (that you have launched the command and will wait for it to finish) and end the turn.\n DO NOTHING ELSE."}
